CAT = "fihygPersonal"; SEG = "PER"
Q = [
("How often should a cook wash their hands during a shift?",
 "Whenever their task changes", ["Only at the start of work", "Only once every hour", "Only when gloves tear"],
 "Ruokavirasto lists starting work, changes between tasks, after raw or soiled food, after the toilet, after smoking and after coughing or blowing the nose. Hands are washed at each of these points."),
("A cook has just used the toilet. What must they do before returning to food work?",
 "Wash their hands carefully", ["Put on gloves without washing", "Wipe hands on the apron", "Use hand sanitiser only"],
 "Hands carry gut bacteria and viruses after the toilet. Washing with soap and water removes them; hand sanitiser does not reliably destroy norovirus."),
("After which task should hands be washed before touching ready-to-eat food?",
 "Handling raw meat", ["Reading a recipe", "Opening a door with gloves on", "Fetching dry spices"],
 "Washing between tasks, especially after raw ingredients or soil-covered or spoiled food, stops bacteria moving to food that will not be cooked."),
("A worker has just blown their nose. What should they do next?",
 "Wash their hands", ["Carry on preparing salad", "Put on a hat", "Wipe hands on a cloth"],
 "Coughing, sneezing and nose blowing put bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus on the hands. Wash hands before going back to food."),
("Which equipment belongs at a proper hand-wash basin?",
 "Liquid soap, paper towels", ["Bar soap and a shared towel", "Dish detergent and a sponge", "A nail brush and bleach"],
 "Ruokavirasto's guide lists running hot and cold water, liquid soap and disposable towels with a bin, or a roller towel. A dirty shared cloth towel is not acceptable."),
("A worker has a small cut on a finger. What should they do?",
 "Cover it and wear a glove", ["Leave it uncovered to heal", "Keep handling food bare-handed", "Wrap it in kitchen paper"],
 "A wound can carry Staphylococcus aureus. Ruokavirasto's guidance is to cover it, for example with a plaster, and wear a protective glove over it."),
("When must protective gloves be worn according to Ruokavirasto?",
 "With false nails, rings or a wound", ["Only when handling bread", "Only when serving drinks", "Never, in any kitchen task"],
 "Ruokavirasto says gloves must be used at least when a worker has artificial nails, jewellery or wounds on their hands."),
("What kind of fingernails should a person handling food with their hands have?",
 "Short and clean", ["Long and painted", "Artificial and glued", "Long but scrubbed"],
 "Long or artificial nails collect dirt and microbes and are hard to clean. Work in which hands touch food needs short nails and healthy cuticles."),
("What is the main rule on jewellery when handling unpackaged food?",
 "Avoid wearing jewellery", ["Wear only gold rings", "Wear rings under gloves always", "Jewellery is never a risk"],
 "Moisture and dirt collect under rings, and a piece of jewellery can come loose into food. Workers handling unpackaged food avoid jewellery."),
("Why is a ring a hygiene risk for a cook?",
 "Dirt collects under it", ["It cools the hands down", "It attracts pests to the bench", "It weakens disposable gloves"],
 "Bacteria thrive in the warm, damp space under a ring and handwashing cannot reach it. A stone can also fall into food."),
("What does Ruokavirasto's guide say about false eyelashes in food work?",
 "Not recommended", ["Required with a hairnet", "Allowed only with glitter", "Compulsory for chefs"],
 "False eyelashes can fall into food, so their use is not recommended. They are a physical contamination risk like loose hair."),
("Why are piercings a hygiene risk in food work?",
 "They break the skin", ["They make food taste of metal", "They attract flying insects", "They cool the face too much"],
 "Ruokavirasto's guide notes that piercings break the skin or mucous membrane, which can carry bacteria, and a piece can fall into food."),
("Why must protective clothing be changed and washed often enough?",
 "Dirty clothing soils food", ["The fabric wears out", "The colours fade", "Inspectors expect new clothes"],
 "Protective clothing is there to stop dirt and microbes reaching food. Once it is soiled it does the opposite, so it is changed and washed often enough."),
("What does Ruokavirasto recommend as protective clothing for food work?",
 "Work outfit, hat and work shoes", ["Own jeans and a T-shirt", "A coat worn outdoors", "Sports kit and open sandals"],
 "The recommended protective clothing is a suitable work outfit, a head covering and work footwear, kept clean and used only at work."),
("A kitchen worker steps outside for a moment during the shift. What is advised for their work clothes?",
 "Change or cover them", ["Wear them unchanged", "Shake them off on return", "Take the apron home"],
 "Ruokavirasto's guide suggests changing or covering protective clothing, for example with a separate jacket, when going outside, so outdoor dirt is not brought in."),
("Why must hair be covered when preparing food?",
 "To keep hair out of food", ["To keep the head warm", "To show the job title", "To reduce sweating at work"],
 "Loose hair and skin flakes carry microbes and are a common physical contaminant. A head covering keeps them out of food."),
("Where should a worker change into protective clothing?",
 "In the changing room", ["At the cooking stove", "In the customer toilet", "In the dining room"],
 "Food premises must have a changing area with space to store outdoor clothes, so they are kept apart from work clothes and food."),
("A worker wearing gloves answers the phone. What should they do before touching food again?",
 "Change the gloves", ["Wipe the gloves on the apron", "Rinse the gloves in water", "Carry on as before"],
 "Gloves are changed whenever they have touched anything other than food or food equipment, such as a phone, money, hair or the face."),
("A cashier also serves unpackaged pastries. What does Ruokavirasto's guide advise?",
 "Use tongs or change gloves often", ["Handle both with the same gloves", "Wear no gloves at all", "Let customers serve the pastries"],
 "Money and pastries do not mix well. If gloves cannot be changed often enough, tongs are used, and handwashing and glove changes must be very careful."),
("Where may a food worker smoke during the working day?",
 "Away from food areas", ["Beside the cooker", "In the dry storeroom", "At the serving counter"],
 "Smoking is kept out of food areas, and hands are washed after smoking before returning to food, because hands touch the mouth and cigarettes."),
("Which statement about hand sanitiser is true?",
 "It is no substitute for washing", ["It removes all visible dirt", "It kills norovirus reliably", "It works well on greasy hands"],
 "Alcohol rub does not clean dirty or greasy hands and works poorly on norovirus. Hands are washed with soap and water and may then be disinfected."),
("Which statement about protective clothing is true?",
 "It is worn only at work", ["It may be worn home and back", "It never needs washing", "It may be shared unwashed"],
 "Work clothes collect microbes and are kept for work only. Ruokavirasto's guide allows staff to wash them at home, as long as they are changed and washed often enough."),
("Which statement about coughing while working is true?",
 "Turn away, then wash hands", ["Cough into the food tray", "Cough into a glove, carry on", "Coughing is never a risk"],
 "Droplets carry microbes onto food and surfaces. Turn away, cover the cough with a tissue or the upper sleeve, and wash hands afterwards."),
("Which statement about eating at work is true?",
 "Eat only in break areas", ["Snack at the work bench", "Eat from the serving line", "Share food from customers' plates"],
 "Eating in food preparation areas brings saliva, crumbs and hands from mouth to food. Staff eat in a separate break area and wash hands afterwards."),
("What is the correct order for washing hands?",
 "Wet, soap, rub, rinse, dry", ["Soap, dry, wet, rinse, rub", "Rinse, dry, soap, rub, wet", "Rub, dry, soap, wet, rinse"],
 "Wet hands, apply liquid soap, rub all surfaces including thumbs, nails and between fingers, rinse well and dry with a disposable towel."),
("Which part of the hands is most often missed in handwashing?",
 "Fingertips and thumbs", ["The palms", "The backs of the wrists", "The forearms"],
 "Studies of handwashing show thumbs, fingertips and the areas between fingers are missed most. These parts touch food the most."),
("What should a worker do with a used disposable towel?",
 "Put it in the basin's bin", ["Hang it up to reuse", "Leave it on the bench", "Use it to wipe the bench"],
 "Disposable towels are used once. A bin next to the hand-wash basin keeps used towels away from food and surfaces."),
("A worker's eye is inflamed and discharging. What is the risk?",
 "Hands can carry it to food", ["None, eyes are sterile", "Only a risk to the worker", "The food will taste salty"],
 "Infected eyes, ears, skin and wounds carry bacteria that reach food on the hands. A worker with an infection should tell their supervisor before handling food."),
("Which of these is a good habit for a food worker?",
 "Hands kept off face and hair", ["Touching hair while cooking", "Wiping hands on clothes", "Chewing gum over the food"],
 "Hands that touch the face, nose or hair pick up bacteria. Keeping them away, and washing after any contact, protects food."),
("A worker has a boil on the forearm. What should they do?",
 "Report it and cover it", ["Squeeze it before work", "Leave it uncovered to dry", "Ignore it if it does not hurt"],
 "Boils and infected skin are full of Staphylococcus aureus. The worker tells the supervisor, covers it well and may need other duties until it heals."),
("A worker returns from a stomach illness abroad. What is wise before handling food?",
 "Tell the supervisor", ["Start work straight away", "Only drink extra water", "Wear two pairs of gloves"],
 "A stomach illness, especially after travel, can mean salmonella or another infection. The supervisor decides with occupational health what is needed before food work."),
("Why should a cloth towel at a hand basin be changed daily, at least?",
 "Damp cloth grows bacteria", ["It becomes too dry", "It loses its bright colour", "It needs ironing daily"],
 "Damp fabric soon holds bacteria, so a cloth towel that stays in use is changed at least daily, and sooner if dirty. Disposable towels avoid the problem altogether."),
("Which of these is the best way to dry washed hands in a kitchen?",
 "A disposable paper towel", ["The front of the apron", "A shared tea towel", "Shaking them dry over food"],
 "Single-use paper towels dry hands without passing microbes between people. Drying also matters: damp hands spread bacteria more easily."),
("Which statement about wedding rings in food work is true?",
 "They trap dirt like other rings", ["They are always allowed bare", "They protect against bacteria", "They never trap any dirt"],
 "The guidance on avoiding jewellery has no exception for wedding rings. If one is worn, a glove must cover it, as Ruokavirasto's rule on gloves requires."),
("Why are hands washed before disposable gloves are pulled on?",
 "Dirty hands soil the glove surface", ["Wet gloves fit more tightly", "Soap makes gloves last longer", "Gloves tear on dry skin"],
 "Pulling gloves on with dirty hands transfers microbes to the outside of the gloves, which then touch food. Ruokavirasto's guide says hands are washed before gloves go on."),
]
